::: {.doc-subject}

Find an Eulerian circuit in the graph if one exists.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L77766-L77883){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.eulerian_circuit()
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Eulerian circuit](../assets/graphs/eulerian-circuit.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="Two triangles sharing node C, so every node has even degree. The Eulerian circuit starts and ends at C and uses each of the six edges exactly once; edges are numbered in the order the circuit takes them, and the card lists C, E, D, C, A, B, C." width="100%"}

An Eulerian circuit is a closed trail that visits every edge exactly once, starting and
ending at the same node. Uses Hierholzer's algorithm. Nodes may be (and usually are)
visited several times; it is the edges that are each used exactly once.

A circuit exists only when every node has even degree (undirected graphs) or equal
in-degree and out-degree (directed graphs), and all nodes that have edges belong to one
connected component (weakly connected, for directed graphs). If these conditions fail
the method returns `None`, so it doubles as a test; `is_eulerian()` performs the same
check without building the circuit.

Eulerian circuits solve "cover every link once and come back" problems, such as:

- route inspection: street sweeping, snow plowing, or mail delivery along every street
- drawing a figure in one continuous stroke without retracing a line
- sequencing problems that reduce to walking every edge of a graph (e.g. de Bruijn
  graphs in genome assembly)

The circuit starts at an arbitrary node that has edges, and when several valid circuits
exist only one is returned. Isolated nodes are ignored: they don't affect the existence
check and never appear in the circuit. The method does not modify the graph.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [Optional[list[int]]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   List of node IDs forming the Eulerian circuit, or `None` if no circuit exists. The
    circuit starts and ends at the same node, so for a graph with `m` edges the list has
    `m + 1` entries. A graph with no nodes gives an empty list, and a single node with no
    edges gives a one-element list.
:::

## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}

An Eulerian circuit traverses every edge exactly once and returns to its starting
node, and Hierholzer's algorithm (1873) constructs one in $O(E)$ time by repeatedly
stitching sub-circuits together as soon as they close. The construction only works
if one actually exists: every node must have even degree in the undirected case, or
in-degree equal to out-degree in the directed case, and the graph must be connected
on the vertices that carry edges (weak connectedness suffices in the directed
setting). Call `is_eulerian()` first if that's not guaranteed, since this method
simply returns `None` when the preconditions fail. Semi-Eulerian graphs (open
Eulerian trail, no circuit) need a slightly different variant that starts at one of
the two odd-degree nodes; `is_semieulerian()` tests for that case. The circuit
returned here is one of possibly many valid ones.

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~is_eulerian`) 

:   Check if a circuit exists.

[](`~eulerian_path`) 

:   A walk crossing every edge once that may end elsewhere.

[](`~eulerize`) 

:   Duplicate edges until a circuit exists.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

The circuit is the list of nodes visited, ending where it started:

::: {#9b1e389a .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

graph = ec.Graph.from_edges(
    [("A", "B"), ("B", "C"), ("C", "A"), ("C", "D"), ("D", "E"),
     ("E", "C")]
)

[graph.get_label_from_id(n) for n in graph.eulerian_circuit()]
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
['C', 'E', 'D', 'C', 'A', 'B', 'C']
```
:::
:::


Each edge labeled by the step at which the circuit crosses it. It goes around one
triangle, and on returning to `C`, around the other:

::: {#792cecf0 .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
spots = {"A": (0, 2), "B": (2, 2), "C": (1, 1), "D": (0, 0), "E": (2, 0)}
graph.set_node_attrs(
    "pos", {graph.get_id_from_label(v): p for v, p in spots.items()}
)
circuit = graph.eulerian_circuit()
step = {
    graph.find_edge_ids(u, v)[0]: i + 1
    for i, (u, v) in enumerate(zip(circuit, circuit[1:]))
}

graph.set_edge_attrs("step", step).plot(
    layout="pos", edge_label_by="step", label_by="label", height=240
)
```

